The Apalachin meeting (https://www.google.com/search?kgmid=%2Fm%2F059465&q=Apalachin+meeting) (often misspelled as applacan) was a historic summit of the American Mafia held on November 14, 1957, in the rural town of Apalachin, New York. When state police raided the gathering, it shattered the Mafia's secrecy and forced federal law enforcement to officially acknowledge the existence of a nationwide crime syndicate. [1] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apalachin_meeting), [2] (https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/1957-meeting-forced-fbi-recognize-mafiaand-changed-justice-system-forever-180967204/), [3] (https://www.youtube.com/shorts/9XC4LVIAtvI)

Purpose and Attendees

• Organizing the summit. Mob boss Vito Genovese called the meeting at the 58-acre estate of local mobster Joseph "Joe the Barber" Barbara. Genovese wanted to legitimize his newly seized control of the Luciano crime family and divide the territory of recently murdered boss Albert Anastasia. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qKBaDjsHdVM&t=1), [2] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apalachin_meeting)
• Summit agenda. Around 100 top Mafia figures from the United States, Italy, and Cuba gathered to discuss illegal gambling, loansharking, Cuban casino interests, and narcotics trafficking. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_oSCmu4dmXU&t=23), [2] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apalachin_meeting)
• Notable attendees. Major underworld figures at the estate included Vito Genovese, Carlo Gambino, Joseph Bonanno, Joe Profaci, and Russell Bufalino. [1] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apalachin_meeting), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qKBaDjsHdVM&t=1)

The Police Raid

• Suspicious activity. New York State Police Sergeant Edgar Croswell noticed an unusual number of luxury cars with out-of-state license plates parked at Barbara's home, alongside large local hotel reservations and meat deliveries. [1] (https://monmouthtimeline.org/timeline/the-meeting-at-apalachin-the-beginning-of-the-end-for-vito-genovese/), [2] (https://troopers.ny.gov/organized-crime-meeting-broken-troopers), [3] (https://restorationobscura.substack.com/p/the-1957-apalachin-ny-mob-summit)
• The roadblock. Troopers set up a roadblock on the single road leading away from the estate to check the vehicles. [1] (https://troopers.ny.gov/organized-crime-meeting-broken-troopers), [2] (https://restorationobscura.substack.com/p/the-1957-apalachin-ny-mob-summit)
• The escape. When the mobsters realized police were outside, panic broke out and dozens fled on foot through the surrounding woods and fields. Police ultimately detained and identified roughly 60 high-ranking bosses, while several dozen others managed to escape. [1] (https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2013/11/apalachin-meeting-on-this-day-in-1957-the-fbi-finally-had-to-admit-that-the-mafia-existed.html), [2] (https://troopers.ny.gov/organized-crime-meeting-broken-troopers), [3] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qKBaDjsHdVM&t=1)

Historical Impact

• Ending FBI denial. Prior to 1957, FBI Director J. Edgar Hoover publicly denied that a national Mafia existed. The Apalachin raid provided undeniable proof of a coordinated syndicate, forcing Hoover to launch the FBI's "Top Hoodlum Program" to target organized crime.
• Legal aftermath. Twenty attendees were initially convicted of conspiracy to obstruct justice for lying about why they were at the estate, though those convictions were later overturned on appeal.
• Shift in mob power. The fiasco ruined Vito Genovese's reputation among other bosses, paving the way for Carlo Gambino to become the most powerful crime boss in the country. The Mafia also closed its membership "books" until 1976 to prevent further law enforcement infiltration. [1] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apalachin_meeting), [2] (https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/1957-meeting-forced-fbi-recognize-mafiaand-changed-justice-system-forever-180967204/), [3] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_oSCmu4dmXU&t=23), [4] (https://www.youtube.com/shorts/9XC4LVIAtvI)
You can read more about the raid from The Mob Museum or the New York State Police. [1] (https://themobmuseum.org/timeline_events/apalachin-meeting/), [2] (https://troopers.ny.gov/organized-crime-meeting-broken-troopers)
